`required` …) on an object only, and of a type list takes the type its `items` or `properties` are for, so a body a magpie from before sends is taken too (`TestAntigravityItemsOnlyOnArrays`). Google's Schema is an object with a fixed set of fields, so a boolean schema (`items: true`, a property's `true` or `false`) or an `x-` keyword such as MCP's `x-mcp-header` (2026-07-28 spec, SEP-2243) turns the whole request away ("Invalid value at '…items.items' (…aiplatform.master.Schema), true" and 'Unknown name "x-mcp-header" … Cannot find field', han_tiv on Discord). `plainSchema` is an allow-list, so `x-` keys never pass it; a boolean is `{}` for true and left out for false (a false property leaves `required` too, a false `items` is `items {}` with `maxItems 0`). Before typing, `geminiToolTypes` runs `geminiSchemaShapes`, which does the same for the relay's case: true is `{}`, false is left out, `x-` keywords go (a property named `x-…` stays), and `additionalProperties` and the `unevaluated…` keywords keep their booleans; a schema with neither goes byte for byte (`TestAntigravityBooleanSchemaAndXKeys`, `TestPlainSchemaBooleansAndXKeysEverywhere`, `TestGeminiToolTypesDropsBooleansAndXKeys`). What is left is still JSON Schema, so `parametersJsonSchema` (Gemini API, Code Assist, Vertex), which runs after it on a Gemini-named body, takes it as before.
